ce doesn't arrive soon we could  be looking at uh 98 to 100 of this  population around 38 million people  potentially being under the poverty line  by next year so we can uh we can  understand that these discussions  obviously are very important um  and and potentially will have a huge  impact on millions of lives uh inside  afghanistan and a lot of that  international aid dependent on how the  taliban the interim government of the  taliban deals with issues like women's  rights for example and the inclusion of  minorities do we have any more  information about how they might deal  with that in the future  thus far with respect to positions for  women in the interim government or a  future permanent government no  indication of any change there there are  no women of any who hold any portfolios  thus far we heard from the taliban today  with respect to education the education  of women they are saying that women are  free to attend universities and schools  but certainly with respect to tertiary  or edge  university education there must be a  separation of the sexes inside  classrooms we understand that may well  involve some sort of curtain for example  in rooms to keep men and women apart but  of course yeah the international  community is looking for proof that the  taliban sticks to its commitments with  respect to human rights with respect to  for example the freedom of the media as  i say more inclusion of ethnic  minorities in any  permanent government more inclusion of  women  the promise that afghanistan will not be  used by armed groups to launch attacks  on foreign countries  and until according to the international  community thus far that sort of proof  is is given by the taliban then of  course they are holding back billions of  dollars of aid and afghan assets that  are frozen for example in u.s banks or  by the world bank  and it is vital um as we've heard from  the countries uh trying to galvanize a a  a a united  international response to this  hum